nd 40% global is 10.12, so 35% global would interpolate to around 9.5, which aligns with that range. For uniform allocation, the non-monotonicity makes it harder to predict where 35% would land between the 30% and 40% values. Looking at the specific model configurations, I see that kl_sparsity30_s42_v3 shows a hard mask PPL of 7.3 versus TEAL global PPL of 8.98, while d030_kl30_uniform achieves 8.8157 on wikitext2 with uniform allocation. The [9.2, 10.5] range at 35% sparsity is puzzling since it's higher than the 30% uniform result, suggesting the uniform constraint might become problematic at higher sparsity levels when some layers require more than 35% of their neurons to maintain performance.\n\nActually, I'm noticing the 40% uniform PPL of 8.36 is lower than the 30% uniform PPL of 8.82, which shows the non-monotonic behavior I'd expect. So the [9.2, 10.5] at 35% might represent TEAL global allocation rather than uniform, which would fit the pattern better. I'll just work with the metric names as provided and estimate 9.6 for the 35% case based on the trajectory between 30% and 40%. So each sparsity level needs its own trained predictor, meaning I'd need to train new ones at 25% and 35%. Looking at the uniform allocation results, a KL predictor trained at 30% gives 8.82 PPL and at 40% gives 8.36, so interpolating to 35% would be around 8.6—nowhere close to the [9.2, 10.5] range I'm seeing, which suggests something different is happening with the uniform evaluation at that sparsity level.\n\nLet me reconsider what \"uniform\" actually means here. The 35%-trained predictor's masks might be applied uniformly across layers, or this could be referring to TEAL global evaluation instead. Looking at the actual data, I'm seeing different PPL values depending on the masking strategy—per-token hard masks give one result, TEAL global gives another, and uniform allocation gives yet another. I need to trace which metric corresponds to which experiment more carefully.\n\nThe pattern is becoming clearer: TEAL global consistently underperforms compared to uniform allocation for KL predictors at lower sparsity levels. At 35%, the per-token approach yields around 7.8-8.0, uniform sits at 8.5-8.7, and TEAL global lands at 9.3-9.5. The [9.2, 10.5] range matches TEAL global, but the metric name says \"uniform,\" which is creating confusion.\n\nI think the issue is that these metric ranges were defined as hypothetical predictions for the median tier in Phase 1, not as direct interpolations from existing experiments. They represent what I expected to see, not necessarily what the data actually shows, so I should assign values that align with the median tier and acknowledge where uncertainty exists.\n\nLooking at the 35% sparsity case specifically, the median tier assumes a gradual degradation without a sharp inflection point between 30% and 40%. But the uniform PPL prediction of 9.6 at 35% creates a non-monotonic jump that doesn't make physical sense—it's much worse than both 30% (8.82) and 40% (8.36). Either there's an error in the median tier key metrics, or \"uniform_ppl\" means something different than I'm interpreting. I'll move forward with reasonable predictions and flag the inconsistency.\n\nRather than getting stuck on this, I'll write out the YAML predictions using the median tier ranges as guidelines but prioritizing physical consistency. Where a range seems off, I'll pick a value that makes empirical sense and explain the reasoning. Now I'm ready to generate the full predictions for the median tier experiments, starting with the C1 ablation at 50% uniform allocation and working through the sparsity levels.\n\nFor the C1 ablation, BCE trained on KL oracle targets captures most of the mask quality, but its ranking scores are less precise than KL's continuous probability outputs. When forced to exactly 50% sparsity via top-k selection on BCE scores, the marginal neurons near the decision boundary get ranked less accurately, leading to about 11% PPL degradation compared to KL. The 0.91 oracle agreement reflects roughly 9% of per-neuron decisions diverging from KL's choices at that forced sparsity level.\n\nAt 30% sparsity with standard BCE, the budget is generous enough that per-neuron scoring produces reasonable masks, though BCE still lags behind KL's precision on the downstream tasks.\n\nMoving to KL at 25% sparsity, I'm seeing much stronger per-token PPL around 6.7, with uniform PPL at 8.1 and MMLU around 0.43. At this lower sparsity level, KL easily identifies the least important neurons, and the modest PPL improvement versus 30% makes sense since the additional 5% of recovered neurons were only marginally important anyway.\n\nAt 35% sparsity with KL, the per-token PPL reaches around 8.\n\n0 and uniform PPL climbs to 8.6, with TEAL global PPL at 9.6 and MMLU dropping to 0.37. This sits in a transition zone where quality degrades gradually rather than sharply—the boundary between having enough capacity and being constrained isn't a hard cutoff but a smooth shift across the 30-40% range.\n\nFor the LongBench evaluation with the 2048-token predictor, KL at 30% maintains 70-80% of dense performance on single-document QA but drops to 60-70% on multi-document tasks and 50-60% on summarization, since the predictor loses effectiveness when extrapolating to much longer sequences beyond its training distribution.\n\nOn MT-Bench, KL at 30% gives around 5.5-6.0 versus dense's 7.0, and at 50% it falls to 3.5-4.5. Multi-turn instruction following is sensitive to sparsity because it requires precise language generation—at 30% most capability survives but subtle differences in word choice and coherence accumulate, while at 50% the model struggles with instruction adherence overall.\n\nThe RULER synthetic tasks show similar degradation to LongBench, though synthetic long-context problems might actually be more sensitive to sparsity since they lack the semantic redundancy of natural text.\n\nNow looking at mask 