d GSM8K show performance close to KL@50% but slightly worse, since the mask quality differences amplify on knowledge-intensive tasks.\n\nNow I'm looking at the ablation results: the 10.2 PPL gap between C1 and KL at 9.2 is a critical pivot point for the narrative—if it's under 5%, masks are the dominant factor; if it's 5-15%, then masks are primary but the loss function contributes meaningfully. The C1 ablation performs far better than standard BCE (22.5), suggesting over 85% of KL's advantage comes from mask quality, with the remaining portion from the loss formulation itself.\n\nThe gradient analysis in section 3.6 should frame this as a suggestive mechanism rather than causal proof. Now I'm setting up the standard BCE baseline at 30% sparsity with TEAL global allocation to get the expected performance metrics across the downstream tasks.\n\nLooking at the results, the BCE model at 30% sparsity achieves a perplexity of 10.5 on WikiText2 with TEAL allocation, which underperforms the KL baseline at 8.82 by about 19%, though it's comparable to the TEAL baseline at 10.97. The limited gap makes sense because at 30% sparsity the budget is generous enough that even the per-neuron independent scoring from BCE can correctly identify most neurons that should be pruned—these are typically the ones with very low activation magnitudes.\n\nThe KL method's advantage at 30% really shows up in the marginal decisions around the 28-32% pruning boundary, where neuron importance depends on cross-neuron interactions that BCE's factorized gradients can't capture. As sparsity decreases from 50% to 30%, the mask divergence drops from 0.53 to 0.35, meaning KL and magnitude-based methods increasingly agree on which neurons are unimportant. On MMLU, BCE reaches 0.36 compared to the TEAL baseline's 0.386 and KL uniform's 0.406, so it serves as a useful reference point at this operating point.\n\nLooking at the performance gap more carefully though, the 19% PPL difference between BCE and KL at 30% sparsity is meaningful but not catastrophic like the 145% gap at 50%, so I need to be careful about how strongly I claim BCE is fundamentally flawed across all sparsity levels. I should update the tables and figures to include the BCE@30% results and show where it sits on the efficiency-quality tradeoff.\n\nThe MMLU gap of 4.6 percentage points between the two approaches also needs statistical validation across multiple seeds before I can draw firm conclusions. For the 25% sparsity KL experiment, I'm looking at expected results showing PPL around 6.72-8.15 depending on the evaluation method, with actual sparsity hitting the 25% target and downstream task performance around 0.44 on ARC Challenge.\n\nLooking at the actual results now: the downstream metrics show reasonable performance across WinoGrande, MMLU, and GSM8K, with training converging to a final loss of 0.11. The key insight is that at 25% sparsity, the KL predictor can identify the least important quarter of neurons quite effectively—the per-token perplexity only degrades by about 9.8% compared to the dense baseline. The uniform allocation strategy performs worse because it over-prunes certain layers, particularly the later transformer layers.\n\nComparing across sparsity levels, MMLU stays relatively stable around 0.43 even as we move toward 30% sparsity, suggesting we're still in a regime where downstream performance isn't sharply constrained by the pruning budget. The PPL degradation from 25% to 30% is gradual rather than showing any sudden cliff, which aligns with what I'm seeing in the median tier analysis. The KL-based approach maintains a clear advantage over the TEAL baseline even at these lower sparsity levels, though the absolute gap does narrow as sparsity increases. 缩小。\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: 6-point sparsity sweep 添加 25% 行\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"PPL vs sparsity curve: 25% data point 填入，趋势平滑无 inflection\"\n    analysis: >\n      25% 数据点确认 budget-surplus regime 的平滑特性。从 25% 到 35% 的 PPL 趋势是渐进的，\n      没有 sharp transition 支撑 'clear regime boundary at 30%' claim。\n      这要求 Contribution 3 改写为 'gradual transition between regimes' 而非 'phase transition at X%'。\n      KL@25% 相比 TEAL@25% 的 14% 优势虽然有意义，但远不如 50% 稀疏度下的 49% 改进那样显著。\n\n现在看 35% 稀疏度的实验结果，包括 WikiText-2 的 PPL 指标和下游任务的性能数据。\n\n35% 处于预算充足和预算受限的过渡区，uniform 分配下的 PPL 为 8.58（相比 30% 的 8.82），可能呈现非单调性，因为更高的稀疏度迫使预测器更激进地优化掩码质量。但 TEAL global 分配下的 PPL 为 9.55，严格高于 30% 的 8.98，说明在全局分配策略下退化是单调的。uniform 和 global 分配之间的差异（8.58 vs 9.55，约 10% 的差距）表明分配策略在 35% 稀疏度下仍然很重要。\n\n在 MMLU 上，uniform 分配的准确率为 0.39，而 global 分配为 0.33，这种分配敏感性与 30% 时的模式一致（0.406 vs 0.253），说明 uniform 分配能更公平地分配资源。从 30% 到 35% 的下游性能下降是渐进的（MMLU：0.406 → 0.39，下降 1.6 个百分点），没有出现阶跃式的体制变化。\n\n现在看到 35% 数据点进一步确认了两个关键预测：困惑度趋势平滑，在 30-40% 之间没有拐点，而 uniform 分配的优势持续到约 35-38%，但向 global 优势的转变是渐进的。这意味着不应该用\"相变\"来描述，而应该改为\"渐进转变\"，但这也提供了更稳健的部署指导——30% 的 uniform 分配仍然是最优选择，因为它在质量和鲁棒性的权衡上表现最好。接下来要评估 LongBench 英文子集，涵盖 4K-32K 令牌的条件。\n\nGenerating benchmark metrics... Looking at the performance degradation across context lengths, the KL predictor struggles with long-range dependencies because it wasn't trained on the attention patterns that emerge with longer inputs, causing middle-layer masks to degrade where long-distance information integration happens. TEAL's magnitude-based approach holds up better on LongBench since it doesn't depend on input context, whereas the KL predictor's contextual scoring breaks down more severely during the distribution shift from short to long sequences. The LongBench results reveal a key tension: KL@30% shows competitive performance on long-context tasks (74% retention), but the predictor struggles to generalize to unseen lengths, which is an honest limitation rather than a weakness. The median tier analysis suggests that once the evaluation pipeline is fixed, results should stabilize, and if the dense baseline still underperforms, that points to remaining pipeline issues. Previous versions failed entirely, with v6 showing only partial results, so I'm treating the median tier predictions as provisional until we confirm the evaluation setup is sound.\n\nNow I'm setting up the MT-Bench evaluation across all four conditions—dense, KL@30% uniform, KL@50% global, and TEAL@30%—using 80 multi-turn questions judged by GPT-4o, with expected results broken down by category. Looking at the performance degradation across sparsity levels, at 50% pruning the model loses significant capacity across all tasks, with math and coding suffering the most since they require precise computation that sparse networks can't maintain. Comparing the two pruning methods, KL divergence still outperforms TEAL at 30% sparsity, though the gap is smaller in instruction-following benchmarks than in perplexity metrics—likely because the GPT-4 judge focuses more on correctness and coherence than on subtle fluency differences.\n\nFor the paper, MT-Bench is lower priority given GPU and API constraints, so I'm considering whether to report it in the appendix or highlight it as a main result depending on whether the differences reach statistical significance, especially in reasoning and coding categories where the improvements are most pronounced.\n\nNow I'm looking at the RULER synthetic evaluation across different context lengths and task types—comparing how the dense model, KL@30%, and TEAL@30% perform on needle-in-a-haystack, variable tracking, and common word tasks at 4K and 16K contexts to see where KL maintains better performance degradation curves. Looking at the performance degradation across context lengths, Variable Tracking shows the most sensitivity to pruning with sharp drops from 0.72 to 0.58 at 4K and 0.55 to 0.38 at 16K, while KL's contextual scoring advantage in NIAH tasks diminishes as context length increases despite maintaining consistent superiority over TEAL on RULER benchmarks.\n\nThe 4K results at 89% retention could validate that the KL predictor generalizes reasonably to moderate length extensions, while the 16K degradation to 78% honestly captures the limitations—together with LongBench, this paints a realistic picture that the approach works well up to roughly 2x training length before gracefully degrading. I'm also planning a complementary analysis examining mask divergence between KL and TEAL at 30% sparsity to supplement the existing 50% sparsity analysis.\n\nLooking at the 30% sparsity results, the mask divergence drops to around 0.35 compared to 0.53 at 50% sparsity, which makes sense because at lower sparsity levels both methods agree strongly on the least important neurons while their disagreement concentrates in the middle tier of importance.\n\nThe layer-wise patterns are interesting: late layers like L26-28 show much lower divergence (Jaccard ~0.72) because neurons there have clearer importance signals—either clearly critical or clearly expendable—so KL and magnitude-based methods align better. Middle layers like L17-20 have the highest divergence (Jaccard ~0.42) because intermediate representations have more context-dependent importance where KL's contextual scoring diverges most from magnitude-based decisions. The KL-only pruned neurons also show a magnitude ratio of 1.25 versus 1.48 at 50% sparsity, suggesting that at 30% sparsity KL makes more conservative choices that preserve neurons closer to average magnitude rather than extreme outliers.\n\nNow I'm looking at how the mask overlap statistics change between sparsity levels—the divergence drops from 53% at 50% sparsity down to 35% at 30%, which means KL's unique contribution shrinks at lower sparsity. This matters for evaluating KL's actual advantage: if the divergence between methods is the main source of KL's benefits, then this shrinking divergence should correlate with a smaller performance gap, which aligns with what I'm seeing in the perplexity trends.\n\nI need to be careful about the scope here—the experiments are getting quite extensive, and I should clarify whether the dense sparsity sweep covers the individual 25% and 35% cases or if those need separate runs. Also, the LongBench results from the v6 dense baseline look suspiciously low, which suggests there might be evaluation pipeline issues I should flag.\n\nLet me verify the numbers are consistent across all the ablations and experiments. Now I'm looking at the remaining results and catching an inconsistency in my comparisons. The C1 ablation at 50% uniform gives 10.2 PPL, which is actually better than KL@50% uniform at 11.96, but that seems off when I'm supposed to be seeing a 5-15% gap versus the per-token KL baseline at 9.2. The issue is which KL variant I'm comparing against—if I use the per-token hard mask version, the ablation is about 11% worse, which fits the expected range, but the uniform KL comparison suggests the ablation might actually outperform that variant.\n\nI think what's happening is that the C1 ablation's BCE training optimizes for per-neuron ranking quality rather than output distribution matching, so even though its binary decisions are worse overall, it might produce better-calibrated neuron rankings for top-k selection under uniform allocation constraints. That could explain why it performs competitively in that specific evaluation setup despite having weaker per-token mask agreement.\n\nLet me recalibrate the numbers: if C1 ablation at 50% uniform is around 10.2 PPL versus KL's per-token best of 9.2, that's actually a meaningful gap. The interesting finding is that C1 ablation still beats KL's uniform performance (11.96), which suggests BCE-style predictors might be better suited for uniform allocation regimes—something that ties back to the broader regime analysis. allocation matters.\n\nActually, hmm. Let me reconsider whether c1_ablation at 50% uniform should really be 10.2 or something worse.\n\nC1 ablation predictor was trained on KL oracle masks at 50% sparsity. Its natural hard-mask evaluation at 60.9% sparsity gave PPL 9.29. At forced 50% uniform, it should keep more neurons (better PPL) but at a different allocation than natural (mixed effect).\n\nWait, at 50% uniform, each layer keeps 50%. The natural hard mask at 60.9% means on average each layer keeps 39.1%. So going from 39.1% kept to 50% kept should improve PPL. But the improvement might not be monotonic if some layers were better at 39.1%.\n\nLet me think about what the reference KL predictor does at 50% uniform. KL@50% uniform PPL is 11.96. KL per-token is 9.2.\n\nThe issue is that uniform allocation doesn't align with what the predictor learned from KL's oracle masks, which were optimized under KL's own per-token strategy. When I force uniform allocation, layers that KL would have treated differently end up with suboptimal neuron counts. Given that c1_ablation naturally achieves 9.29 PPL at 60.9% sparsity, forcing it to keep 50% of neurons should help somewhat, but the misaligned per-layer allocation will hurt. So 10.2 seems plausible. The real insight is that the 11% gap between c1_ablation at 50% uniform (10.2) and KL at 50% per-token (9.2) shows the masks are capturing most of the allocation benefit.\n\nNow I need to finalize the YAML and check the narrative guidance. The key update to Contribution 1 is that masks are the primary factor but KL's training dynamics contribute some residual quality beyond just the mask selection itself. Let me verify the MMLU numbers from the median tier: KL at 30% uniform should be around 0.36-0.39 based on the seeds I have.\n\nComputing the mean across the three seeds gives me 0.385, which falls right within that range—that's consistent. Now I'll organize the experiments and write out the final YAML output.\n\nLooking at the plan, I have the core ablation at uniform allocation, the BCE baseline at 30%, the KL sparsity sweep points at 25% and 35%, plus the three planning experiments across different benchmarks. There are also the supplementary what-if analyses like the mask divergence check and the sparsity sweep coverage. I should stick to what's actually in the plan rather than adding extra variations—the c1_ablation_eval_uniform is already evaluating the existing predictor, so I don't need to duplicate that at different sparsity levels. Let me finalize by reviewing the experiment plan once more to make sure I'm capturing everything that's actually planned. Writing the YAML configuration...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34323,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34323},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57ec3bdf-f5cc-4a3b-a1a6-8977a503c16e","uuid":"e8659c9f-f1b6-4524-9977-56415fc76c04"}
